PART 5.  THE C1 FILTER ON THE INVERSION: WHAT CAN MOTION BETRAY?
==============================================================================
   Gemini: "watch the motion, it betrays the constraints." C1 filters this
   claim into three verdicts -- one per ledger category. Executed on a single
   observed trajectory of the oscillator, with the observer given ONLY the
   trajectory (no equations, no parameters):

   SELECTOR (the orbit label E):
     recovered from the trajectory alone: 0.844989
     true value:                          0.845000
     error: 1.06e-05   -> RECOVERABLE. the motion betrays it.

   FORCED (the law -- here, the frequency):
     period recovered from zero-crossings: 6.282222
     true 2*pi:                            6.283185
     error: 9.63e-04   -> RECOVERABLE. the motion betrays it.

   SELECTED (the point on the orbit -- the phase):
     phase range swept by the motion: [-3.141, +3.139]
     fraction of the full circle visited: 0.999
     -> the motion SWEEPS EVERY PHASE. There is no constraint holding it.
        Asking 'what wall set the phase?' returns NULL -- correctly. It is
        GAUGE. NOT RECOVERABLE, and that is not a failure of the method.

   VERDICT ON THE INVERSION (the C1 filter, not a check of anyone's arithmetic):

     FORCED   content -> RECOVERABLE from motion. (it is what makes motion legal)
     SELECTOR content -> RECOVERABLE from motion. (it is conserved BY the motion)
     SELECTED content -> NOT recoverable, because it constrains nothing. Legal
                         motion sweeps it freely. An inversion that "finds" a
                         constraint here has hallucinated a wall.

   So: "the motion betrays the constraint" is TRUE, exactly two-thirds of the
   time, and the failure mode is precise and nameable. That is the strongest
   form of the claim that survives C1 -- and it is stronger than the loose form,
   because it comes with the one warning label that keeps it from being used to
   invent walls that are not there.

